<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-coordinates" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Coordinates and Change of Basis</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-coordinates">
    <title>Coordinates Relative to a Basis</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-coordinates-unique">
      <title>Coordinates Are Unique</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ \vec{b}_i \}_{i=1}^{n}</m> be a basis for a vector space
          <m>V</m>. Then for each <m>\vec{x} \in V</m>, there exists a unique set of
          scalars <m>\{ c_i \}_{i=1}^{n}</m> such that
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} c_i \vec{b}_i.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <definition xml:id="def-coordinate-vector">
      <title>Coordinate Vector and Coordinate Mapping</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>coordinates of <m>\vec{x}</m> relative to the basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}</m></term> are denoted by
          <md>
            [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} c_1 \\ c_2 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          and the mapping <m>\vec{x} \mapsto [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m> is known as the
          <term>coordinate mapping</term>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-coordinates-two-bases">
      <title>The Same Vector in Two Coordinate Systems</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}</m> be the standard basis of
        <m>\R^2</m> and let <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ \vec{b}_1, \vec{b}_2 \}</m> be a second
        basis, related to the first by
        <md>
          \vec{e}_1 = \tfrac{1}{2}\vec{b}_1,
          \qquad
          \vec{e}_2 = \tfrac{1}{2}\vec{b}_1 + \vec{b}_2.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        A vector <m>\vec{x}</m> with
        <m>[\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, i.e.,
        <m>\vec{x} = \vec{e}_1 + 2\vec{e}_2</m>, has
        <md>
          [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_2} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{3}{2} \\ 2 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad \text{i.e.} \qquad
          \vec{x} = \tfrac{3}{2}\vec{b}_1 + 2\vec{b}_2.
        </md>
        The vector <m>\vec{x}</m> has not moved; only the grid we measure it against has
        changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Solving the two relations above for <m>\vec{b}_1</m> and <m>\vec{b}_2</m> gives
        <md>
          \vec{b}_1 = 2\vec{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \vec{b}_2 = \vec{e}_2 - \vec{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
        </md>
        so <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> rules the plane with a grid of parallelograms rather than
        squares.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-two-coordinate-grids">
        <caption>
          The same vector <m>\vec{x}</m> measured against two different bases. Its
          coordinates change because the grid changes, not because the vector moves. In each
          panel the two scaled basis vectors are laid tip to tail, and <m>\vec{x}</m> is
          the diagonal of the parallelogram they span.
        </caption>
        <sidebyside widths="44% 44%" margins="auto">
          <figure xml:id="fig-standard-grid">
            <caption>
              In <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>: <m>\vec{x} = \vec{e}_1 + 2\vec{e}_2</m>, so
              <m>[\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </caption>
            <image xml:id="img-standard-grid">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-standard-grid">
                <diagram dimensions="(350,300)" margins="8">
                  <coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-1.7,3.6,3.6)">
                    <group at="standard-grid">
                      <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                      <group at="parallelogram">
                        <line endpoints="((0,0),(0,2))" stroke="#777" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((0,2),(1,2))" stroke="#777" thickness="1"/>
                      </group>
                      <line at="arrow-e1" endpoints="((0,0),(1,0))" stroke="orange" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="arrow-e2" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="orange" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="arrow-2e2" endpoints="((1,0),(1,2))" stroke="blue" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="vector-x" endpoints="((0,0),(1,2))" stroke="red" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(0.5,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-10)" scale="1.2" color="orange"><m>\vec{e}_1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(0,0.5)" alignment="w" offset="(-10,0)" scale="1.2" color="orange"><m>\vec{e}_2</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(1,1)" alignment="e" offset="(10,0)" scale="1.2" color="blue"><m>2\vec{e}_2</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(1,2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,10)" scale="1.2" color="red"><m>\vec{x}</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </coordinates>
                  <annotations>
                    <annotation ref="standard-grid" text="The vector x as the diagonal of a parallelogram built from the standard basis">
                      <annotation ref="arrow-e1" text="The standard basis vector e 1 points one unit along the x axis, and is the first component of x"/>
                      <annotation ref="arrow-e2" text="The standard basis vector e 2 points one unit along the y axis"/>
                      <annotation ref="arrow-2e2" text="Twice e 2 is laid tip to tail from the end of e 1, rising two units to reach the point 1 comma 2"/>
                      <annotation ref="parallelogram" text="Faint lines complete the parallelogram whose sides are e 1 and twice e 2"/>
                      <annotation ref="vector-x" text="The vector x is the diagonal of that parallelogram, so x equals e 1 plus 2 e 2 and its coordinates in this basis are 1 and 2"/>
                    </annotation>
                  </annotations>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
          </figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-skew-grid">
            <caption>
              In <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>: <m>\vec{x} = \tfrac{3}{2}\vec{b}_1 + 2\vec{b}_2</m>,
              so <m>[\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_2} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{3}{2} \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </caption>
            <image xml:id="img-skew-grid">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="pf-skew-grid">
                <diagram dimensions="(350,300)" margins="8">
                  <coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-1.7,3.6,3.6)">
                    <group at="skew-grid">
                      <group at="lattice">
                        <line endpoints="((-3,-1),(4,-1))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((-3,1),(4,1))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((-3,2),(4,2))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((-3,3),(4,3))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((-2,-2),(-8,4))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((0,-2),(-6,4))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((2,-2),(-4,4))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((4,-2),(-2,4))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((6,-2),(0,4))" stroke="#8cc" thickness="1"/>
                      </group>
                      <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
                      <group at="parallelogram">
                        <line endpoints="((0,0),(-2,2))" stroke="#777" thickness="1"/>
                        <line endpoints="((-2,2),(1,2))" stroke="#777" thickness="1"/>
                      </group>
                      <line at="arrow-32b1" endpoints="((0,0),(3,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="arrow-b1" endpoints="((0,0),(2,0))" stroke="green" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="arrow-b2" endpoints="((0,0),(-1,1))" stroke="green" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="arrow-2b2" endpoints="((3,0),(1,2))" stroke="blue" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <line at="vector-x" endpoints="((0,0),(1,2))" stroke="red" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                      <label anchor="(1,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-10)" scale="1.2" color="green"><m>\vec{b}_1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(-0.7,0.7)" alignment="sw" offset="(-6,-8)" scale="1.2" color="green"><m>\vec{b}_2</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(2.7,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-10)" scale="1.2" color="blue"><m>\tfrac{3}{2}\vec{b}_1</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(2,1)" alignment="e" offset="(10,0)" scale="1.2" color="blue"><m>2\vec{b}_2</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(1,2)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,10)" scale="1.2" color="red"><m>\vec{x}</m></label>
                    </group>
                  </coordinates>
                  <annotations>
                    <annotation ref="skew-grid" text="The same vector x as the diagonal of a parallelogram built from the basis B 2">
                      <annotation ref="lattice" text="A grid of parallelograms spanned by b 1 and b 2, replacing the square grid"/>
                      <annotation ref="arrow-b1" text="The basis vector b 1 equals 2 e 1 and points two units along the x axis"/>
                      <annotation ref="arrow-b2" text="The basis vector b 2 equals e 2 minus e 1 and points up and to the left"/>
                      <annotation ref="arrow-32b1" text="Three halves of b 1 reaches the point 3 comma 0, and is the first component of x"/>
                      <annotation ref="arrow-2b2" text="Twice b 2 is laid tip to tail from the end of three halves b 1, running up and to the left to reach the point 1 comma 2"/>
                      <annotation ref="parallelogram" text="Faint lines complete the parallelogram whose sides are three halves b 1 and twice b 2"/>
                      <annotation ref="vector-x" text="The vector x is the diagonal of that parallelogram, so x equals three halves b 1 plus 2 b 2 and its coordinates in this basis are three halves and 2"/>
                    </annotation>
                  </annotations>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-change-of-basis">
    <title>The Change of Basis Matrix</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-change-of-basis">
      <title>Change of Basis Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2, \ldots, \vec{u}_n \}</m> and
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ \vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \ldots, \vec{v}_n \}</m> be two
          bases of a vector space. Then to change the basis with respect to which the
          vector <m>\vec{x}</m> is written from <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> to
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>, we can use the <term>change of basis matrix</term>
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> as
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_1}
            = [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_2},
          </md>
          where
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
                [\vec{u}_1]_{\mathcal{B}_2} \amp [\vec{u}_2]_{\mathcal{B}_2} \amp \cdots
                \amp [\vec{u}_n]_{\mathcal{B}_2}
              \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-change-of-basis">
      <title>Computing a Change of Basis Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Continuing <xref ref="eg-coordinates-two-bases" text="type-global" />, find
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> and verify it on
          <m>\vec{x}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          In the above example, we have <m>\vec{e}_1 = \frac{1}{2}\vec{b}_1</m> and
          <m>\vec{e}_2 = \frac{1}{2}\vec{b}_1 + \vec{b}_2</m>, which means
          <md>
            [\vec{e}_1]_{\mathcal{B}_2} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            [\vec{e}_2]_{\mathcal{B}_2} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \text{and} \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \amp \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For instance, with
          <m>[\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \amp \frac{1}{2} \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{3}{2} \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = [\vec{x}]_{\mathcal{B}_2}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
